.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt CONSOLE 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm console ,
.Nm tty ,
.Nm ptmx ,
.Nm pts
.Nd console and pseudoterminals
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/console
.Pp
.Pa /dev/tty
.Pp
.Pa /dev/ptmx
.Pp
.Pa /dev/pts/N
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Pa /dev/console
is the kernel console.
Output goes to the screen and COM1 serial port.
The kernel gives the first process descriptors 0, 1 and 2 on the console,
each open for reading and writing, as Linux gives init
.Pa /dev/console
opened
.Dv O_RDWR ;
a program such as tmux writes through its standard input.
Keyboard and serial input pass through one terminal line discipline.
When the boot line names a port of a virtio console,
.Sy console= Ns Cm hvc0 ,
that port carries what this page says of COM1, the output and the
input; see
.Xr hvc 4 .
The kernel's screen log level is controlled through
.Pa /dev/screenlog ;
see
.Xr bootlog 4 .
.Pp
.Pa /dev/tty
opens the caller's controlling terminal, either the console or a
pseudoterminal slave.
A caller without a controlling terminal, one that gave it up with
.Dv TIOCNOTTY
among them, gets
.Er ENXIO .
.Pp
When the leader of the session the console controls exits, or a
.Xr vhangup 2
hangs it up, every description of the console opened through
.Pa /dev/tty
is hung up: a read returns 0 and a write fails with
.Er EIO .
A call already under way on such a description ends too: a read returns
0, or the bytes it has copied, and takes none of the input typed after
the hangup; a write returns the bytes it has written, or fails with
.Er EIO ;
and
.Xr poll 2
reports
.Dv POLLHUP
even to a caller that asked for no events.
The descriptions of
.Pa /dev/console ,
and the descriptors 0, 1 and 2 the kernel gives the first process, are
not, as Linux's hangup passes over
.Pa /dev/console ,
so that init and the shells it starts again keep their console.
.Pp
Opening
.Pa /dev/ptmx
allocates a pseudoterminal pair and returns its master.
An open with
.Dv O_CREAT Ns | Ns Dv O_EXCL
returns
.Er EEXIST
without allocating a pair.
The slave is
.Pa /dev/pts/N ,
where N is the lowest index not used by a live pair.
A network login uses one pair per session.
Master writes become slave input.
Master writes are serialized: a write waits while another is in
progress, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when non-blocking.
A master write that fills the input returns the bytes it fed.
One that has fed no byte waits for room, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
when non-blocking.
The master's TCIOFF and TCION wait for the same write lock even on a
non-blocking descriptor; a signal that interrupts the wait gives
.Er EINTR .
With software flow control enabled, a flow character in the waiting
input can act at once if the input queue has room to retain the prefix
through it; the write takes and returns that prefix before any wait.
Slave output and echo enter the master's 4096-byte ring.
A slave writer the ring has no room for waits until the master reads, as
on Linux, or, when non-blocking, returns what fit or fails with
.Er EAGAIN ;
.Xr poll 2
reports the slave writable only while the ring has room.
One write reaches the ring whole before another writer's begins, as
Linux's terminal write lock keeps it, so two processes' lines are not
mixed within a write; a non-blocking writer that finds another write in
progress fails with
.Er EAGAIN .
Echo that arrives while the ring is full is held in the discipline's
echo buffer, whose oldest byte is discarded when that buffer fills too.
The slave has the same line discipline and job control as the console.
.Xr stat 2
on the slave gives the second it last returned input to a reader as its
access time and the second it last took output as its modification
time, from which
.Xr w 1
reports a session's idle time.
The pair is freed after both ends close, and its index may be reused.
.Ss Line discipline and job control
Canonical mode collects input into lines; raw mode permits input without
waiting for a complete line.
A canonical line holds 4095 bytes before its newline, as on Linux; a byte
typed past them is dropped and counted in
.Li line-drops
of
.Pa /proc/console .
A read returns at most one line, and the whole of it when its buffer is
large enough.
The input queue holds 8192 bytes, room for two such lines.
With
.Dv IUTF8 ,
which a new terminal has, the erase character removes a whole UTF-8
character and rubs out as many cells as the screen drew it in: two for a
CJK character or an emoji, and none for a combining mark.
In raw mode a read waits as
.Dv VMIN
and
.Dv VTIME
say,
.Dv VTIME
in tenths of a second.
With both 0 it returns at once with what is there.
With
.Dv VMIN
above 0 and
.Dv VTIME
0 it waits for
.Dv VMIN
bytes.
With
.Dv VMIN
0 and
.Dv VTIME
above 0 it returns with the first byte, or returns 0 once
.Dv VTIME
has passed since the read began.
With both above 0 it waits for a first byte, then returns when
.Dv VMIN
bytes have come or
.Dv VTIME
has passed since the last of them.
A read for fewer bytes than
.Dv VMIN
waits only for as many.
.Xr poll 2
does not wait for
.Dv VTIME :
it reports the terminal readable with
.Dv VMIN
bytes when
.Dv VMIN
is above 0 and
.Dv VTIME
is 0, and otherwise with one byte, as Linux does.
Echo and signal generation are controlled by the terminal settings.
With signal generation enabled, the interrupt and suspend characters,
normally Ctrl-C and Ctrl-Z, send
.Dv SIGINT
and
.Dv SIGTSTP
to the foreground process group, and discard the unread input and the
output not yet shown, unless
.Dv NOFLSH
is set.
With
.Dv IXON ,
which a new terminal has, Ctrl-S stops the terminal's output and Ctrl-Q
restarts it, and neither reaches a reader; with
.Dv IXANY
any key restarts it.
A write waits while output is stopped, Scroll Lock and its LED are on,
and the Hold key, Scroll Lock, stops and restarts output, as on Linux.
.Dv TCXONC
does the same from a program, as
.Xr ioctl 2
describes.
.Pp
A background process reading its controlling terminal receives
.Dv SIGTTIN .
A background write generates
.Dv SIGTTOU
only when
.Dv TOSTOP
is set.
A blocked or ignored
.Dv SIGTTIN
fails the read with
.Er EIO ,
and a blocked or ignored
.Dv SIGTTOU
lets the write go ahead, as on Linux.
A write that would post
.Dv SIGTTOU
from an orphaned process group, which no member's parent in the
session can continue, fails with
.Er EIO
instead.
.Ss Screen
The screen is a terminal whose escape sequences the terminfo entry
.Ql rach2
lists, of 80 columns and 25 rows on the VGA's text mode, and on a
framebuffer of as many as the screen holds; its window size, which
.Dv TIOCGWINSZ
reports, is the screen's.
Text other than ASCII is read as UTF-8 and drawn from the console's
font.
At boot the kernel loads into the adapter a font that keeps code page
437's box drawing, shades and blocks and has the Cyrillic letters of
Russian, Kyrgyz, Kazakh, Ukrainian and Belarusian, from GNU Unifont, and
uses it when the adapter reads it back; otherwise the adapter's own font,
code page 437, stays.
When the loader leaves a framebuffer, as GRUB does under UEFI and by BIOS
when its menu asks for one, the kernel draws the console on it in the
screen's own mode, in that font with code page 437's ASCII and box
drawing, each character 8 pixels by 16 times the largest whole number at
which the screen still holds 80 columns and 24 rows, so that each pixel of
the font is a sharp square, and as many characters as fit: 85 columns and
24 rows at 16 pixels by 32 on a 1366 by 768 screen, and 80 by 25 at that
size on a 1280 by 800 one.
The cursor is two lines at the foot of its cell.
A Latin letter with an accent that the font lacks is drawn without it,
punctuation as its ASCII likeness, and the glyphs coding agents draw
their screens with as the nearest the font has: a bullet as a bullet,
a spinner's star as
.Ql * ,
braille as
.Ql \&. ,
a pointer as
.Ql > ,
the blocks and quadrants as a half or full block, a check mark as
.Ql v
and a cross as
.Ql x .
Any other character the font lacks is drawn as one
.Ql \&? .
A wide character, CJK or an emoji, takes two cells, its glyph or
.Ql \&?
and a blank, and begins the next line rather than start in the last
column; a combining mark takes none, and the letter under it is drawn
alone.
.Ql "ESC [ ? 1 h"
and
.Ql "ESC [ ? 1 l"
set and reset the cursor keys' application mode, and
.Ql "ESC ="
and
.Ql "ESC >"
the keypad's;
.Ql "ESC c"
resets both and clears the screen.
Alt with a key sends ESC before the key's character, as Meta does on
Linux's console.
The screen answers the queries a VT102 answers, and the answer is the
console's input, read as typed bytes:
.Ql "ESC [ c"
with
.Ql "ESC [ ? 6 c" ,
.Ql "ESC [ 5 n"
with
.Ql "ESC [ 0 n" ,
and
.Ql "ESC [ 6 n"
with the cursor's position,
.Ql "ESC [ row ; column R" .
Operating system commands, device control strings and application
strings, which a program sends to learn a terminal's features, are
read to their end and not drawn.
A terminal on the serial port gives its own answers too.
.Ss Blanking
The screen goes dark after ten minutes without a key pressed or the mouse
moved, and the next key or move shows it again with what it showed.
Output written meanwhile is drawn when it comes back; the serial port
is not blanked.
Blanking paints the screen black, through the VGA's palette in text mode
and with black pixels on a framebuffer, and turns every backlight off;
see
.Xr backlight 4 .
.Pp
The kernel command line's
.Ql consoleblank=N
sets the time to N seconds, and
.Ql consoleblank=0
keeps the screen from blanking, as on Linux.
The escape
.Ql "ESC [ 9 ; N ]" ,
which
.Ql setterm -blank N
writes on Linux, sets it to N minutes, at most 60, and 0 for never,
counts the time from then and shows a screen it blanked.
.Pp
The screen does not blank, and no backlight changes, while a program draws
through
.Pa /fb
or a DRM master, such as a Wayland compositor, holds the display, as
Linux leaves a console in graphics mode alone.
A program that takes the screen shows a blank one first, and the time
counts again from when the console has the screen back.
.Pp
Each blank and its end are recorded in the kernel's log, with their count
since boot and their cause:
.Ql console: blanked: 1, after the time without input
and
.Ql console: unblanked: 1, by a key or a mouse move ,
or
.Ql by the escape
or
.Ql on request .
.Ss Drawing through /fb
A program takes the screen by opening
.Pa /fb ,
as
.Xr open 2
describes, and owns it until it asks for text mode, closes
.Pa /fb
or exits.
A descendant may open its own descriptor to take the screen in turn.
The former owner receives a
.Dv FB_EVENT_LOST
event and cannot draw until
.Dv FB_EVENT_REGAINED
reports its return.
Events are read as one whole
.Vt struct fb_event
and make
.Xr poll 2
report
.Dv POLLIN ;
O_NONBLOCK gives EAGAIN when none waits.
Events are shared by the owner's open descriptions.
Its mode, palette, keyboard settings and console termios are restored
before return, and it redraws its whole screen.
Dead suspended owners are skipped.
The last owner's return restores the console's original settings, flushes
unread input and redraws its saved text.
Fork and dup share an open description; only its last close releases it.
Separate opens by the same owner keep it until all close.
While a program owns the screen the console draws nothing on it and the
screen does not blank.
A game that wants the keyboard's scan codes puts the keyboard in
.Dv K_RAW
and the terminal in raw mode, as below, and reads them from the console.
The open enters mode 13h, 320 by 200 pixels of a byte each through a
palette of 256 colors, which a framebuffer shows as tall as the screen at
the picture's own 320:200, centered, and the VGA shows itself.
On a framebuffer that is RGB at 15, 16, 24 or 32 bits a pixel, the
request
.Dv FB_MODE_NATIVE
gives the owner the whole screen at its own size and pixel format,
which
.Dv FB_GETINFO
reports, and the owner writes rows of pixels at byte offsets from the top
left pixel with
.Xr pwrite 2 ,
or with
.Xr lseek 2
and
.Xr write 2 .
The requests are in
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
and the header for programs is
.In rach2/fb.h .
.Ss DRM masters
The console is the in-kernel client of the first card under
.Pa /dev/dri
that sets modes, as Linux's fbdev emulation is.
A process that becomes the card's DRM master, by its first open of the
card or by
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_SET_MASTER ,
takes the screen and the keyboard from the console: the console draws
nothing, the program that owns
.Pa /fb
reads
.Dv FB_EVENT_LOST
and its writes wait, or fail with
.Er EAGAIN
on a nonblocking descriptor, an open of
.Pa /fb
by another program fails with
.Er EBUSY ,
and the keyboard is in
.Dv K_OFF ,
so that keys typed meanwhile reach no program.
Bytes received on COM1 still reach the console, and neither the hand-over
nor the return discards unread input.
.Dv KDGKBMODE
then reports, and
.Dv KDSKBMODE
sets, the mode that returns.
.Pp
The console takes them back when the last open DRM file closes.
.Dv DRM_IOCTL_DROP_MASTER
leaves the scanout intact so that another open file can take the role
without a console mode set between them.
On a native driver it sets a mode on each connected display, scanned out
from a buffer of its own, and draws its text there again.
The mode is the size that
.Ql video= Ns Ar W Ns x Ns Ar H
names on the kernel's command line, else the size of the screen the
loader left, else the display's preferred mode.
On the firmware's framebuffer, as
.Xr fbdrm 4
drives it, it turns the card's output off and draws on the firmware's
screen again.
The keyboard's mode returns, the owner of
.Pa /fb
reads
.Dv FB_EVENT_REGAINED
and redraws its whole screen, and its writes go on.
A display connected or disconnected while no master holds the card moves
the console to the new modes in the same way.
.Pp
The console keeps the columns and rows it had at boot.
On a screen of another size it draws them at the largest whole scale of
the font at which they fit, centered, and on one too small for them at
scale 1 it shows those that fit from the top left corner.
Until the card first has no open files after a master used it, the
console stays where it was at boot: on the loader's framebuffer, or on the VGA's text mode, which it
then leaves for the card's buffer.
.Ss Terminal requests
The console and pseudoterminal slaves answer these
.Xr ioctl 2
requests:
.Bl -tag -width TIOCSPGRP
.It Dv TCGETS
Read the terminal settings.
.It Dv TCSETS , TCSETSW , TCSETSF
Set the terminal settings.
.Dv TCSETSF
also discards unread input.
.It Dv TCGETS2
Read the terminal settings with the speeds their baud codes name.
.It Dv TCSETS2 , TCSETSW2 , TCSETSF2
Set the terminal settings as
.Dv TCSETS ,
.Dv TCSETSW
and
.Dv TCSETSF
do; the speeds are not kept.
.It Dv TCFLSH
Discard unread input for selectors 0 and 2.
Selector 1 does nothing, and selector 2 discards unread input only;
queued pseudoterminal output is unchanged.
.It Dv TIOCGPGRP
Read the foreground process group ID.
The console must be the caller's controlling terminal.
.It Dv TIOCGSID
Read the session ID; the console must be the caller's controlling
terminal.
.It Dv TIOCSPGRP
Set the foreground process group.
The terminal must be the caller's controlling terminal, and the group
must belong to that session.
.It Dv TIOCSCTTY
Make this terminal the session's controlling terminal.
The caller must be a session leader, and the terminal must not belong
to another session.
.It Dv TIOCGWINSZ
Read the terminal window size.
.It Dv TIOCNOTTY
From the leader of the session the console controls, send the foreground
group
.Dv SIGHUP
and
.Dv SIGCONT
and detach the console from the session; from another member, detach it
from the caller's process alone, which then has no controlling terminal.
The console must be the caller's controlling terminal.
.El
.Pp
The console also answers Linux's
.Dv TIOCLINUX ,
whose argument points to a byte, the subcode, from a process whose
session the console controls or from root:
.Bl -tag -width TIOCSPGRP
.It 14
Blank the screen now, until subcode 4; a key does not show it.
.It 4
Show the screen if it is blank.
.It 15
Return 1 while the screen is blank and 0 otherwise.
.El
.Pp
Another subcode fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
and a caller that is neither fails with
.Er EPERM .
.Ss Keyboard
The console answers Linux's keyboard requests of
.In linux/kd.h ,
with Linux's numbers, structures and errors, which the tools of kbd,
.Xr loadkeys 1 ,
.Xr dumpkeys 1 ,
.Xr setleds 1 ,
.Xr kbd_mode 1 ,
.Xr showkey 1
and
.Xr kbdrate 8 ,
send.
A pseudoterminal answers none of them, with
.Er ENOTTY .
.Bl -tag -width KDGKBDIACRUC
.It Dv KDGKBTYPE
Store
.Dv KB_101
in the byte the argument points to.
.It Dv KDGKBMODE , KDSKBMODE
Read into an int, or set from the argument's value, the keyboard's mode:
.Dv K_UNICODE ,
the mode at boot, and
.Dv K_XLATE
translate keys into characters;
.Dv K_RAW
gives the keyboards' set-1 scancodes, as a PS/2 keyboard sends them,
a held key's repeats as make codes;
.Dv K_MEDIUMRAW
gives Linux's key codes, a code below 128 as one byte with 0x80 set on
its release and a higher one as three, 0x00 or 0x80, then the code's
high and low seven bits each with 0x80 set;
.Dv K_OFF
gives nothing, and is the mode while a DRM master holds the screen, as
.Sx DRM masters
describes.
In the last three modes the lock keys, the scrollback keys and the
kernel shell's Ctrl-C do nothing, and bytes received on COM1 are
discarded, except while a DRM master holds the keyboard in
.Dv K_OFF ;
the key bytes pass through the line discipline, whose
settings the program sets itself.
Setting a mode discards unread input and stops a held key's repeat.
.It Dv KDGKBMETA , KDSKBMETA
Read into an int, or set, how Alt reaches a program:
.Dv K_ESCPREFIX ,
ESC before the key, at boot, or
.Dv K_METABIT .
.It Dv KDGKBLED , KDSKBLED
Read into a byte, or set, the lock flags
.Dv K_SCROLLLOCK ,
.Dv K_NUMLOCK
and
.Dv K_CAPSLOCK ,
with their defaults in bits 4 to 6.
.It Dv KDGETLED , KDSETLED
Read into a byte the LEDs lit,
.Dv LED_SCR ,
.Dv LED_NUM
and
.Dv LED_CAP ,
or light them; the LEDs follow the lock flags until
.Dv KDSETLED
sets them, and again after a value with a higher bit set.
.It Dv KDGKBENT , KDSKBENT
Read or set one entry of the keymap through a
.Vt struct kbentry .
The keymap has 256 tables of 256 entries; a table that does not exist
reads as
.Dv K_NOSUCHMAP
at key 0 and
.Dv K_HOLE
elsewhere, is made on its first set, and is freed by
.Dv K_NOSUCHMAP
set at key 0, except table 0.
A typed entry beyond its type's range fails with
.Er EINVAL .
Unlike Linux, which reads and sets a Unicode entry only in
.Dv K_UNICODE ,
the console reads one in every mode but
.Dv K_XLATE ,
where it reads as
.Dv K_HOLE ,
and sets one in every mode, so that a keymap loads while a terminal
emulator holds the keyboard in
.Dv K_MEDIUMRAW .
.It Dv KDGKBSENT , KDSKBSENT
Read or set a function key's string through a
.Vt struct kbsentry ;
a string set must end within its 512 bytes, else
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv KDGKBDIACR , KDSKBDIACR , KDGKBDIACRUC , KDSKBDIACRUC
Read or replace the accent table, in 8 bits or in code points; a code
point above 255 reads in 8 bits as 0xFF.
A table set has at most 255 entries, else
.Er EINVAL .
.It Dv KDKBDREP
Set the repeat's delay and period in milliseconds from a
.Vt struct kbd_repeat ,
leaving a time of 0 or less as it is, and store there the times in
force; 250 and 33 at boot.
.It Dv KDGETMODE
Store in an int
.Dv KD_GRAPHICS
while a program draws through
.Pa /fb ,
and
.Dv KD_TEXT
otherwise.
.El
.Pp
The requests that set the mode, the meta setting, the lock flags and the
LEDs are open to a process of the session the console controls, the
process that holds
.Pa /fb ,
and root; those that set the keymap, the strings, the accents and the
repeat to root alone.
Another caller fails with
.Er EPERM ,
and a bad pointer with
.Er EFAULT .
.Ss Speaker
.Dv KIOCSOUND
sounds the PC speaker until the next request and
.Dv KDMKTONE
sounds a tone of a given length, which the kernel ends, each with a
divisor of the interval timer's 1193182 Hz, in Linux's encodings; see
.Xr ioctl 2 .
Where the HD Audio codec has a beep generator, as on the Lenovo G50-70,
which has no PC speaker of its own, each request sounds it too, at the
.Cm speaker
level of
.Xr mixer 4 .
The console's own BEL does not sound the speaker;
.Xr term 1
sounds its bell with
.Dv KDMKTONE .
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/console
.It Pa /dev/console
Character device, major 5, minor 1; mode 0666.
.It Pa /dev/tty
Character device, major 5, minor 0; mode 0666.
.It Pa /dev/ptmx
Character device, major 5, minor 2; mode 0666.
.It Pa /dev/pts/N
Pseudoterminal slave N, owned by the user who opened the master, of the
group
.Ql tty ,
5, with mode 0620, as Linux's devpts gives a slave with
.Ql gid=5,mode=620 ,
so that
.Xr write 1
and
.Xr wall 1 ,
which run as that group, may write to it and
.Xr mesg 1
may refuse them.
.El
